Strategy: // CSD_RATIONALE_BEGIN
// The strict always-constrained-inside policy achieved perfect syntax, but the
// evaluation failures show a different problem: it often closed the SQL span at
// the first parser-complete prefix, yielding locally valid yet semantically
// truncated SQL such as a complete subquery followed by stray closing markers.
// For Spider, many prefixes are grammar-complete before the intended query is
// semantically finished, so "close immediately on complete prefix" is too eager.
// 
// This revision keeps the strong parser-respecting behavior inside the SQL span
// but changes the stopping policy. Outside the span, it still advances one token
// at a time and opens on the first token containing "<<". Inside the span, it
// uses `ConstrainedStep` exclusively, but when the current constrained prefix is
// complete it does not immediately close. Instead, it asks the parser how many
// valid next tokens remain. If there are still continuations, it keeps decoding;
// only when the prefix is complete and there are no valid continuations does it
// close the span. This directly targets the observed premature-closure failures.
// 
// To avoid pathological dead ends or local loops, the strategy also uses parser
// queries as guards: if the active constrained prefix is no longer a narrowable
// live prefix, it rolls back to the longest valid prefix before continuing. This
// keeps the constrained state aligned with the grammar without ever sampling
// unconstrained tokens inside SQL. The result is a "late close, rollback on dead
// end" policy: still syntax-safe, but much less eager to terminate the query.
// CSD_RATIONALE_END
// CSD_PROOF_SKETCH_BEGIN
// parser_validity: In the outside-span branch, either EOS is produced and we
//   break, or one unconstrained token is appended. If it opens a span, we set
//   `currentConstrainedOut := []`, which is a valid parser prefix by the method
//   precondition; otherwise we remain outside and the implication is vacuous. In
//   the rollback branch, `RollbackToValidPrefix` returns a valid parser prefix
